title: BunnyMark
intro: A benchmark: how many bouncing bunnies this computer keeps up at sixty frames a second, and how steadily.
project: BunnyMark | Bunnies

chapter: The benchmark | Bunnies, and a script that counts them
select: BunnyMark
explain: component:BunnyMark | The BunnyMark script | It adds bunnies while the game keeps up sixty frames a second. When the game falls behind, it takes the last lot away and tries fewer, until it has the count.
explain: field:BunnyMark.Growth | Growth | Each step adds half as many bunnies again, and the step halves every time the game falls behind.
explain: field:BunnyMark.Precision | Precision | The run ends once a step would add less than this. For a quick look, a rougher count will do.
set: BunnyMark.Precision = 0.2
set: BunnyMark.Window = 0.5
explain: field:BunnyMark.HoldSeconds | Hold Seconds | Then it holds the count it found and times every frame: the frame rate, its one percent low, which shows any frame that missed the screen's beat, and how many times the garbage collector ran.
set: BunnyMark.HoldSeconds = 3
explain: field:BunnyMark.HoldCount | Hold Count | Or it holds a count you give it straight away, to check that many bunnies keep up.

chapter: A run | Counting bunnies
play
wait: 3
explain: game | Bunnies pour in | Each bunny is a sprite and a short script, copied from a prefab: an ordinary entity, just like a game's. The frame rate and the count show in the corner.
wait: 3
explain: game | Big scenes | Each frame draws every bunny as it comes to it, in one pass, then puts the draws back to front by their keys, moving as few as it can. It calls each script only for the steps it has, and draws the sprites in a few big batches, making no garbage. An exported game keeps fifty thousand bunnies at sixty frames a second.
wait: 6
explain: game | The count | When it has found the count and held it, it shows it along the bottom with how the hold went, and saves it with the best so far.
tap: Space
wait: 2
explain: game | Again | Space starts a new run.
stop
say: Exported games are measured the same way: a script in the tools folder builds a game from this template, plays it, and prints the count with the hold's frame rate, one percent low and garbage collections, so changes to the engine can say how much faster they made it.
